  state.\n+- `WorkLeafSession`, which contains agent id, kind, feature/title, transcript lines, loading state,\n+  and lifecycle status.\n - `WorkLeafEvent`, which reports session creation, session updates, streamed lines, selection\n   changes, transcript lines, and quit requests.\n - `WorkLeafLoading`, which distinguishes launch and waiting-for-reply states.\n+- `WorkLeafSessionStatus`, which distinguishes active sessions, sessions awaiting feature-done\n+  confirmation, and locally closed completed sessions.\n \n `WorkLeafEvent` uses append-oriented transcript events for efficient remote frontends. `AgentAdded`\n provides the initial session snapshot, `AgentLineAppended` carries one new session line, and\n `CommandTranscriptLine` carries one new command transcript line. `AgentStatusUpdated` carries\n-session metadata and loading state without re-sending the session transcript. `AgentUpdated` remains\n-part of the DTO surface for full-session replacement when an integration needs it. Session line\n-appends and status changes are not paired with full-session replacement events, so remote frontends\n-can update long transcripts without re-receiving the full transcript text.\n+session metadata, loading state, and lifecycle status without re-sending the session transcript.\n+`AgentUpdated` remains part of the DTO surface for full-session replacement when an integration needs\n+it. Session line appends and status changes are not paired with full-session replacement events, so\n+remote frontends can update long transcripts without re-receiving the full transcript text.\n \n New UIs should consume `WorkLeafController` and these DTOs. They should not duplicate worker\n spawning, session naming, review lookup, loading bookkeeping, or orchestrator event routing.\n@@ -288,10 +295,10 @@\n `/`, and enters insert mode so `/status`-style input submits through the same selected-agent chat\n path.\n \n-The terminal app maps a session to a left-pane `READY` marker when the controller exposes no loading\n-state for that session. `TerminalUi` queues one terminal bell when a chat transitions into the ready\n-state and renders ready rows in reverse video so they remain highlighted until the chat becomes busy\n-again.\n+The terminal app maps an active session to a left-pane `READY` marker when the controller exposes no\n+loading state for that session. Locally closed completed sessions are not shown as ready.\n+`TerminalUi` queues one terminal bell when a chat transitions into the ready state and renders ready\n+rows in reverse video so they remain highlighted until the chat becomes busy again.\n \n `src/ui.rs::TerminalUi` owns terminal-specific presentation state:\n \ndiff --git a/docs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md b/docs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md\nindex 430e6d75bdc1c69c68a2ed071a21ebeea12d9bf6..8ca81eae2fc50819e2c1d1ea588b045f760f68e2\n--- a/docs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md\n+++ b/docs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md\n@@ -84,10 +84,12 @@\n 5. The orchestrator runs a review agent for that patch.\n 6. If the review agent emits `@work-leaf` directives, the orchestrator resolves those directives for\n    the review agent before treating reviewer output as findings.\n-7. If there are no findings, the user can mark the review-agent chat as done.\n+7. If there are no findings, the patch-agent chat asks the user whether the feature is done.\n 8. If there are findings, the orchestrator sends those findings to the corresponding patch agent.\n 9. The patch agent keeps patching through the orchestrator until the review agent reports no\n    findings.\n+10. A yes response to the feature-done prompt closes the patch-agent chat locally; a no response\n+    leaves it active, and later chat input reopens a closed patch-agent chat.\n \n The current implementation path for this review loop builds patch-agent review targets through\n `src/review.rs::GitHistory`, asks the original agent for a summary, launches or resumes the patch\n@@ -406,7 +408,10 @@\n Reviewer `@work-leaf` directives are resolved in the reviewer conversation before output is\n interpreted as findings. If the reviewer finds issues, the orchestrator sends those findings to the\n patch agent. The patch agent then continues through the configured read path and patch protocol. When\n-the reviewer reports no findings, the review chat can be marked done by the user.\n+the reviewer reports no findings, the reviewed patch-agent chat asks the user whether the feature is\n+done and includes the reviewed commit hash prefix in the prompt. A yes response closes the patch-agent\n+chat locally. A no response leaves the chat active. Non-yes/no chat input clears the confirmation\n+state, reopens the session when needed, and is sent to the backend agent.\n \n ## Developer Path\n \n@@ -434,7 +439,8 @@\n 13. The review agent can request file text through the orchestrator before reporting findings.\n 14. If the review agent reports findings, the orchestrator sends them to the patch agent and the patch\n     agent keeps patching.\n-15. If the review agent reports no findings, the user can mark the review chat as done.\n+15. If the review agent reports no findings, the reviewed patch-agent chat asks whether the feature\n+    is done.\n 16. Reviewed work from the current command-chat or controller instance can then be linearized into the\n     final history.\n \n"},"emittedAtMs":1787836596205}
